[i0001] I've picked up i0001, done the analysis, and parked a question on the card. Stopping here as the workflow requires.
[i0001] **What I found** (recorded in the card's Notes, committed as `28c210e`):
[i0001] The paddle binds both sticks in `src/ship/mod.rs:101`, and `ship_articulate` (`src/ship/mod.rs:140`) uses the stick `y` twice — for paddle tilt and for forward/back position — so a Y sign flip would both mirror the tilt and drive the paddle the wrong way. That matches the reported hunch.
[i0001] The device-dependence is entirely inside gilrs; `bevy_gilrs`' converter is a 1:1 passthrough. On macOS `gilrs-core` sets `IS_Y_AXIS_REVERSED = true`, but `gilrs` only applies that flip to axes it has already *resolved* as `LeftStickY`/`RightStickY`/`DPadY` via the bundled `SDL_GameControllerDB`. An unresolved axis falls through as `Axis::Unknown` and is **not** flipped — so a pad the DB doesn't match arrives Y-inverted relative to one it does. That's a concrete mechanism for exactly this symptom.
[i0001] Matching is by SDL GUID (vendor/product/version). The DB has `Mac OS X` entries for the Pro 2 only under vendor `c82d` / product `0660`, and the Pro 2 enumerates with a *different* vendor/product per position of the mode switch on its back (S mode → Switch Pro Controller, X mode → Xbox pad, …).
[i0001] I also confirmed an upstream limitation worth knowing: gilrs parses the SDL `~` invert suffix (`mapping/parser.rs:201`) but never applies it — `Token::AxisMapping.inverted` is dead code. It doesn't affect the macOS 8BitDo entries (no `~`), but it would break any mapping that needs one.
[i0001] **Why I stopped:** the four candidate root causes (unmatched pad / mis-mapped pad / Y-only inversion / deadzone drift) need different fixes and are indistinguishable without the controller attached. The card asks which mode switch position the pad is in and what precisely misbehaves — and offers to build an opt-in input-debug overlay first if reading raw axis values is easier than describing the symptom.
[i0001] Nothing is pushed. The commit contains only the card file; the rest of the pre-existing dirty tree is untouched.
